course: dictionary
lesson: 13713
title: Who starts it?
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To instigate means to start something or make it happen. It is a formal word, so you will often see it in news stories, school reports and rules. «The head teacher instigated a new reading club.» The head teacher started the club.
explain: Instigate can also mean to stir up trouble, often from behind the scenes. «Who instigated the fight?» asks who started the trouble. The person who instigates may not be the one throwing the punches. They may be the one who encouraged it.
explain: Look at the words around it. Words like «plan», «new rules» or «fight» tell you what is being started or stirred up. A good reader asks: who began this, and did they do it openly or quietly?
explain: The noun is instigator, the person who starts something. «At his instigation» means because he urged it. Knowing who instigated something helps us see who is responsible, which matters for fairness.
fact: Instigate means to start something, or to stir up trouble, often while staying in the background.
fact: Instigate is a formal word, so it is common in news and school reports.
fact: An instigator is the person who starts something.
fact: «At his instigation» means because he urged it.
fact: A good check is to swap in «started»: «Who started the fight?» still makes sense, so you have the right idea.
exercise: «The head teacher instigated new rules for the playground.» What does instigated mean here? | Ate quickly | Wrote a poem about | *Started or brought in | Lost or forgot
exercise: «Who instigated the fight at lunchtime?» What is this question asking? | Who was hurt | Who won the fight | *Who started the trouble | Who ate the most lunch
exercise: Ravi asks, what is an instigator? | A person who is always late | A person who sings loudly | A person who cleans up the mess | *A person who starts something
test: «Tom stayed behind the wall, but he instigated the argument.» What does instigated mean? | *Stirred up | Calmed down | Lost | Tidied up
test: «The class captain instigated a new recycling plan.» What does this sentence tell you? | The captain was asked to leave | The captain forgot the plan | The captain ended the plan | *The captain started the plan
exam: «The plan was made at her instigation, because she urged the others to act.» What does «at her instigation» mean? | After she left | *Because she urged it | Without her knowing | Against her wishes
